    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
      <!-- 1. Antoine Vapor Driving Force -->
      <div class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-5">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-sky-100 dark:bg-sky-950/60 text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-300 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-sm mb-3">
          1
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Antoine Vapor Pressure &amp; Mass Transfer</h3>
        <p class="mb-2">
          Membrane distillation (MD) is a non-isothermal separation process driven by the vapor pressure difference across a hydrophobic microporous barrier. The saturation vapor pressure of water at the membrane-liquid interfaces is calculated using the Antoine equation:
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono bg-surface p-2.5 rounded-lg border border-hairline text-[11px] text-ink mb-2">
          P_sat(T) = exp(23.1964 - 3816.44 / (T - 46.13))<br />
          J_w = C_MD · [ a_w · P_sat(T_f,m) - P_sat(T_p,m) ]
        </div>
        <p>
          Where <em>a_w</em> is the water activity coefficient, heavily depressed at higher Brix levels by Raoult-Kelvin thermodynamic effects, reducing the effective driving force as juice thickens.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 2. Cantor-Laplace Capillary Pore Wetting -->
      <div class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-5">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-sky-100 dark:bg-sky-950/60 text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-300 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-sm mb-3">
          2
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Cantor-Laplace Pore Wetting Dynamics (LEPw)</h3>
        <p class="mb-2">
          The critical barrier preventing bulk juice from flooding through the pores into the distillate stream is governed by the Liquid Entry Pressure of water (<em>LEP_w</em>):
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono bg-surface p-2.5 rounded-lg border border-hairline text-[11px] text-ink mb-2">
          LEP_w = (-2 · B · γ_L · cos θ) / r_max<br />
          Breakthrough Risk: ΔP_hydraulic &gt; LEP_w
        </div>
        <p>
          Where <em>γ_L</em> is liquid surface tension, <em>θ</em> is water contact angle (&gt;110°), <em>r_max</em> is maximum pore radius (0.1–0.45 µm), and <em>B</em> is the pore geometry factor. Natural organic surfactants (saponins, essential oils, lipids) depress surface tension, drastically lowering <em>LEP_w</em> and requiring superhydrophobic materials.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 3. Boundary Layer Polarizations & Sanitary Standards -->
      <div class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-5">
